✅ static Keyword in Java
The static keyword in Java means that the member belongs to the class rather than to any specific object. It can be used with:

Variables

Methods

Blocks

Nested classes

🔹 1. Static Variable
Shared by all objects of the class (only one copy).

java
Copy
Edit
class Student {
    static String school = "ABC School";
}
🔹 2. Static Method
Can be called without creating an object.

Can't access non-static variables directly.

java
Copy
Edit
class Demo {
    static void greet() {
        System.out.println("Hello from static method");
    }
}
Usage:

java
Copy
Edit
Demo.greet(); // No object needed
🔹 3. Static Block
Runs once when the class is loaded.

java
Copy
Edit
class Example {
    static {
        System.out.println("Static block called");
    }
}
🔹 4. Static Class (Nested only)
Can be created inside another class (as a static nested class).

java
Copy
Edit
class Outer {
    static class Inner {
        void show() { System.out.println("Static inner class"); }
    }
}
⚠️ Important Notes:
static methods can't use this keyword.

You can call static members using ClassName.member.

